- **6 sections** (theory · theory · dataTable · code · quiz · sandbox), 12 min, module `m4`.
- Theory 1 frames the move from binary mask → contour list (Suzuki–Abe 1985), inlines the canonical Bikesgray Wikimedia image, and warns about the *boundaries-not-regions* misconception. Theory 2 explains the `(1, N, 4)` `[Next, Previous, First_Child, Parent]` row layout and the `RETR_*` / `CHAIN_APPROX_*` axes.
- DataTable compares the four retrieval modes (EXTERNAL / LIST / CCOMP / TREE) — what each returns, what the hierarchy looks like, when to use it.
- Code exercise: `summarize_hierarchy(hierarchy)` returning `{outer, holes}` from a list of `[next, prev, child, parent]` rows — 4 tests (1 visible smoke + 3 hidden), full reference `solution`.
- Quiz uses the *findContours returns regions* misconception as the prompt with three plausible distractors (per-region masks, connectedComponents-style label image, list of bounding boxes).
- Sandbox runs `cv2.findContours` on a synthetic mask (rect-with-hole + separate square) across all four `RETR_*` modes and prints the hierarchy rows side-by-side.
- 4 lesson-level sources (OpenCV findContours tutorial · OpenCV hierarchy tutorial · Suzuki–Abe 1985 paper · Gonzalez & Woods Ch. 11); section-level sources attached to each theory section + the quiz where they directly support the claim.
